@startuml
title Threads: One Request, Then Shutdown (--io-threads above 1)

' How one request crosses threads, and the order in which the threads stop.
' With --io-threads 1 the keyspace thread's part runs on the I/O thread
' itself, in line, and there is no hand-off.

actor Client
participant "I/O thread\n(session strand)" as IO
participant "Keyspace thread\n(Executor)" as KS
participant "Watchdog thread" as WD
participant "Main thread" as Main

== One request ==
Client -> IO : RESP request
IO -> IO : read, parse_pending()
IO -> KS : Executor::run_on(batch)
activate KS
KS -> KS : CommandScope: stamp the heartbeat\n(start time, generation, name)
KS -> KS : run the command on the keyspace
loop every 100 ms, whatever the other threads do
  WD -> WD : heartbeat(): has a command been\nrunning past --watchdog-ms?
end
opt the command is still running past it
  WD -> WD : warn, once per command\n(ReportPolicy::Once)
end
KS -> KS : CommandScope ends: clear the heartbeat
KS --> IO : continues_on(session strand)
deactivate KS
IO -> Client : the batch's replies, in one write

== Shutdown ==
[-> IO : SIGTERM: the signal wait completes
note over IO
  The race in serve() ends: the accept loop,
  cron and the watchdog are asked to stop.
end note
IO -> WD : stop request
WD -> WD : the cancelled wait completes\non the watchdog thread
WD --> IO : continues_on(io_): back to the event loop
note over IO
  A work guard has kept io_.run() going
  this long: io_ had no work of its own left.
end note
IO -> IO : stop the listener, drain the sessions,\nthen io.stop()
IO --> Main : io_.run() returns; workers joined
Main -> KS : Executor::stop(): wait for queued batches
Main -> Main : final save, then exit

@enduml
